WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.715
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll run

00:00:03.715 --> 00:00:06.554
a JavaFX Task, a unit of work with a reported

00:00:06.554 --> 00:00:09.141
outcome, on a background thread, keep the

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:12.107
interface responsive, and handle completion and

00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:14.567
cancellation with a fresh task for each

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:15.198
execution.

00:00:15.847 --> 00:00:18.826
These skills let applications keep accepting

00:00:18.826 --> 00:00:21.738
input while work is pending, present useful

00:00:21.738 --> 00:00:24.650
outcomes, and stop unfinished work when its

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.072
owning window closes.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.625
A campus staff member will start a controlled

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:30.328
count task.

00:00:30.328 --> 00:00:33.011
It deliberately waits for permission, then

00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:33.778
returns six.

00:00:33.778 --> 00:00:36.589
The wait gives us time to check that another

00:00:36.589 --> 00:00:39.656
button still responds; the small return value is

00:00:39.656 --> 00:00:41.125
not a performance test.

00:00:41.773 --> 00:00:44.483
We should see a waiting status while Check

00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:47.516
response still updates its counter, followed by

00:00:47.516 --> 00:00:49.840
Result six after permission arrives.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.808
Then we'll change the task's setup and predict

00:00:52.808 --> 00:00:54.873
how its completion path changes.

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:58.521
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open Main

00:00:58.521 --> 00:01:00.927
dot Java and build the task and window.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:03.766
We'll keep the waiting operation separate from

00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:06.234
the callbacks that update live controls.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.223
First, bring in the JavaFX application, task and

00:01:13.223 --> 00:01:16.215
window types, plus the bounded queue that will

00:01:16.215 --> 00:01:17.907
carry a permission signal.

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:20.965
The queue will carry permission, not the result

00:01:20.965 --> 00:01:21.225
six.

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.980
Define ControlledCountTask as a Task whose

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.073
successful result is an Integer.

00:01:52.073 --> 00:01:55.082
Give it the shared permission queue and a flag

00:01:55.082 --> 00:01:57.634
that lets us deliberately test failure.

00:01:57.634 --> 00:02:00.512
Constructing this task stores its inputs; it

00:02:00.512 --> 00:02:02.082
does not start the work.

00:02:28.746 --> 00:02:31.400
Next, supply the background work in call.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.508
It waits for one permission item, checks whether

00:02:34.508 --> 00:02:37.486
cancellation was requested, and either reports

00:02:37.486 --> 00:02:39.946
the controlled problem or returns six.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.334
The protected override implements the Task's

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:07.085
work method.

00:03:07.085 --> 00:03:09.463
The wait belongs on the worker thread.

00:03:09.463 --> 00:03:12.468
A cancellation request can interrupt the waiting

00:03:12.468 --> 00:03:15.159
queue operation, and the cancellation check

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:17.976
avoids ordinary work after a request has been

00:03:17.976 --> 00:03:18.539
observed.

00:03:18.539 --> 00:03:21.105
No statement here changes a live control.

00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:24.046
Add a separate click counter for the interface.

00:03:24.046 --> 00:03:26.582
Only its button callback will update this

00:03:26.582 --> 00:03:27.077
counter.

00:03:27.077 --> 00:03:29.860
Its purpose is to make responsiveness visible

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.839
while the task is still waiting.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:51.699
Now create the window session: one capacity-one

00:03:51.699 --> 00:03:54.385
queue, one task, one named worker, and one

00:03:54.385 --> 00:03:55.536
interface counter.

00:03:55.536 --> 00:03:57.710
Start with the failure flag false.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.780
The thread name identifies this example during a

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.466
separate cleanup check; it does not affect

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:04.169
scheduling.

00:04:42.026 --> 00:04:45.158
Add the Ready status, the response counter and

00:04:45.158 --> 00:04:46.043
four buttons.

00:04:46.043 --> 00:04:48.971
Allow finish and Cancel task begin disabled

00:04:48.971 --> 00:04:51.626
because no worker has been started yet.

00:05:19.236 --> 00:05:22.140
Wire Start task to disable itself, enable the

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.109
two pending-work actions, describe the waiting

00:05:25.109 --> 00:05:27.045
session, and start the worker.

00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:30.013
The callback requests background execution and

00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:32.659
returns; it does not wait for the task to

00:05:32.659 --> 00:05:33.111
finish.

00:05:52.397 --> 00:05:55.220
Starting a Thread is different from calling its

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.422
run method directly.

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:59.185
A direct call here would place the task's wait

00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:01.888
on the interface thread and could prevent the

00:06:01.888 --> 00:06:04.291
very button action needed to release it.

00:06:04.291 --> 00:06:07.114
A Task and Thread each represent one execution,

00:06:07.114 --> 00:06:09.397
so repeated Start must not reuse them.

00:06:09.463 --> 00:06:12.616
Let Allow finish try to offer one signal without

00:06:12.616 --> 00:06:14.193
waiting for queue space.

00:06:14.193 --> 00:06:16.689
Disable it only if the offer succeeds.

00:06:16.689 --> 00:06:19.382
Then let Check response increment its own

00:06:19.382 --> 00:06:21.813
counter and update the counter label.

00:06:44.257 --> 00:06:46.973
Offer returns promptly with a boolean result.

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:49.628
False means the signal was not inserted; the

00:06:49.628 --> 00:06:51.619
interface does not wait for room.

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:54.154
The interface can send permission and keep

00:06:54.154 --> 00:06:56.688
receiving events while the worker waits or

00:06:56.688 --> 00:06:57.231
proceeds.

00:06:57.231 --> 00:06:59.645
We do not join the worker or wait for an

00:06:59.645 --> 00:07:01.757
unfinished result in this callback.

00:07:01.853 --> 00:07:04.877
Add the cancellation request and the successful

00:07:04.877 --> 00:07:06.100
completion handler.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.995
When success arrives on the interface thread,

00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:11.697
read the available result, display it, and

00:07:11.697 --> 00:07:14.078
disable actions that no longer apply.

00:07:33.645 --> 00:07:35.629
Give failure its own handler.

00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:38.435
Read the recorded exception's message and

00:07:38.435 --> 00:07:40.556
display a clear failure report.

00:07:40.556 --> 00:07:43.157
A problem should not be presented as a

00:07:43.157 --> 00:07:45.620
successful empty or invented result.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.751
Give cancellation its own displayed outcome.

00:08:06.751 --> 00:08:09.915
Also request cancellation when the owning window

00:08:09.915 --> 00:08:12.881
is hidden, so closing a window does not leave

00:08:12.881 --> 00:08:14.925
this permission wait unmanaged.

00:08:31.685 --> 00:08:33.444
Cancellation is cooperative.

00:08:33.444 --> 00:08:36.460
Our wait responds to interruption, but arbitrary

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.414
work can continue if it ignores a stop request.

00:08:39.414 --> 00:08:42.430
A Canceled label or a closed window alone is not

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:44.189
proof that the worker ended.

00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:47.017
We keep the visible outcome separate from the

00:08:47.017 --> 00:08:48.085
completion check.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.882
Arrange the controls, show the window and

00:08:50.882 --> 00:08:53.726
provide the JavaFX application entry point.

00:08:53.726 --> 00:08:56.569
Task ready will confirm that the window was

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:59.546
created; the task result will appear later in

00:08:59.546 --> 00:09:00.670
the status label.

00:09:34.368 --> 00:09:37.229
Run the complete program and move to Desktop.

00:09:37.229 --> 00:09:40.282
We will start the task, use Check response twice

00:09:40.282 --> 00:09:43.143
during its wait, and then allow it to finish.

00:10:00.984 --> 00:10:03.246
The task is waiting for permission.

00:10:03.246 --> 00:10:06.220
The response counter changes when we click, so

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:09.193
another interface callback can run during that

00:10:09.193 --> 00:10:09.516
wait.

00:10:09.516 --> 00:10:12.360
The work and the live controls have separate

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.459
responsibilities.

00:10:16.561 --> 00:10:19.661
Permission lets call return six, and the success

00:10:19.661 --> 00:10:22.566
handler displays that result on the interface

00:10:22.566 --> 00:10:23.018
thread.

00:10:23.018 --> 00:10:25.860
Start remains disabled because this task and

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.797
thread have already been used.

00:10:27.797 --> 00:10:30.444
Close this window before creating another

00:10:30.444 --> 00:10:30.961
session.

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:39.610
Keep the task, window and handlers the same, but

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:41.526
change the failure flag to true.

00:10:41.526 --> 00:10:44.340
Before running the new session, predict whether

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.974
Check response can still work while the task

00:10:46.974 --> 00:10:49.668
waits and which completion handler should run

00:10:49.668 --> 00:10:50.686
after permission.

00:11:09.439 --> 00:11:12.359
Run the changed program, start its fresh task,

00:11:12.359 --> 00:11:15.343
check the response counter and then allow it to

00:11:15.343 --> 00:11:15.787
finish.

00:11:15.787 --> 00:11:18.771
Compare the observed result with the prediction

00:11:18.771 --> 00:11:20.739
before changing the code again.

00:11:37.118 --> 00:11:39.556
The interface still responds during the

00:11:39.556 --> 00:11:40.556
controlled wait.

00:11:40.556 --> 00:11:43.056
Once permission arrives, call throws the

00:11:43.056 --> 00:11:45.806
controlled problem, and the failure callback

00:11:45.806 --> 00:11:47.118
displays its message.

00:11:47.118 --> 00:11:50.118
The success handler does not supply a result for

00:11:50.118 --> 00:11:51.493
this failed execution.

00:11:54.332 --> 00:11:57.288
Restore the failure flag to false and create a

00:11:57.288 --> 00:11:58.188
fresh session.

00:11:58.188 --> 00:12:01.272
This time, start the task and cancel it while it

00:12:01.272 --> 00:12:03.907
is still waiting, before allowing finish.

00:12:31.313 --> 00:12:34.212
The canceled callback supplies its own outcome

00:12:34.212 --> 00:12:36.292
and disables the pending actions.

00:12:36.292 --> 00:12:39.317
This is cancellation, not a successful result of

00:12:39.317 --> 00:12:39.633
zero.

00:12:39.633 --> 00:12:42.217
Closing the window and checking that this

00:12:42.217 --> 00:12:44.738
program actually exits provides separate

00:12:44.738 --> 00:12:47.763
completion evidence for this controlled example.

00:12:50.593 --> 00:12:53.338
Open another fresh session, start the task and

00:12:53.338 --> 00:12:55.187
check that the window responds.

00:12:55.187 --> 00:12:57.574
Then close it while it is still waiting.

00:12:57.574 --> 00:12:59.662
The hidden-window callback requests

00:12:59.662 --> 00:13:01.929
cancellation; check process completion

00:13:01.929 --> 00:13:04.793
separately from the disappearance of the window.

00:13:20.775 --> 00:13:23.816
Run the baseline again to create new task and

00:13:23.816 --> 00:13:24.830
thread objects.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.871
Starting and allowing this new session should

00:13:27.871 --> 00:13:29.763
recover the baseline result.

00:13:29.763 --> 00:13:32.871
Reopening is a new execution, not a restart of

00:13:32.871 --> 00:13:33.750
the old task.

00:13:52.453 --> 00:13:54.384
The fresh session returned six again.

00:13:54.384 --> 00:13:56.264
The background task owns its waiting

00:13:56.264 --> 00:13:58.717
computation, and the interface owns its control

00:13:58.717 --> 00:13:59.135
updates.

00:13:59.135 --> 00:14:01.640
A nonblocking handoff keeps the callbacks short;

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.728
separate completion handlers explain the

00:14:03.728 --> 00:14:04.146
outcome.

00:14:04.146 --> 00:14:06.443
When you adapt this pattern, which operation

00:14:06.443 --> 00:14:08.948
could wait, where must it run, and what evidence

00:14:08.948 --> 00:14:11.454
will show that closing its window also ended its

00:14:11.454 --> 00:14:12.028
owned work?
